<text>if (se->in_sync_count || se->out_sync_count) {
    drm_dbg(&v3d->drm, "Two multisync extensions were added to the same job.");
    return -EINVAL;
}</text>
<text>// The unbounded loop in v3d_get_extensions that iterates: while (ext) { ... ext = ext->next; }</text>
Reason:
The guard only rejects a multisync extension when either sync count is nonzero. A self-referential extension with both counts set to zero passes this check, returns 0, and the loop in `v3d_get_extensions` continues without any bound. Because the `next` pointer points to itself, the loop never terminates, causing an infinite loop that pegs a CPU core indefinitely.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>if (!multisync.in_sync_count && !multisync.out_sync_count) {
    drm_dbg(&v3d->drm, "Empty multisync extension\n");
    return -EINVAL;
}</text>
Reason:
This new check explicitly rejects a multisync extension where both `in_sync_count` and `out_sync_count` are zero. An empty multisync carries no synchronization information and serves no legitimate purpose. By returning `-EINVAL`, the calling loop exits with an error, breaking the infinite loop and preventing the attack.

